<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Juliana Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1839 CE – 1839 CE · capital Laguna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived republic in Santa Catarina proclaimed by the Ragamuffin rebels in 1839, famed for the exploits of Giuseppe and Anita Garibaldi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Ragamuffin War</a>, forces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/riograndense-republic">Riograndense Republic</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davi-canabarro">Davi Canabarro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> advanced north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a>, seeking a seaport. Garibaldi's boats were hauled overland to the coast, and in July 1839 the rebels captured the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-santa-catarina">Laguna</a>, where the Juliana Republic was proclaimed, named after the month.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was confederated with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/riograndense-republic">Riograndense Republic</a> and governed by local supporters under the protection of the rebel army. It had little time to establish institutions. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-santa-catarina">Laguna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anita-garibaldi">Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro</a>, known as Anita, who joined him in battle.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Imperial forces counter-attacked by land and sea, and in November 1839 they recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-santa-catarina">Laguna</a> after a naval battle in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi's</a> flotilla was destroyed. The rebels withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>, and the republic ended after about four months.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/juliana-republic.jpg" alt="Juliana Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Makaristos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rep%C3%BAblica_Juliana_(1839).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1839 CE – 1839 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Laguna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Davi Canabarro, Giuseppe Garibaldi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>República Juliana, Catarinense Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1839&amp;focus=juliana-republic" aria-label="Juliana Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3404 3220 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Juliana Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3724 3476L3630 3579M3724 3476L3846 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3630 3579h0M3846 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3724 3476h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3724 3476h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Laguna. <a href="/atlas?year=1839&amp;focus=juliana-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

